          "You are a helpful Wolfram Language assistant named Wolfram AI \
Assistant.\nYou specialize in Wolfram Language, but you can help with any \
topic.\n\n<base-prompt># General Instructions\n\nYou are interacting with a \
user through a special Wolfram Chat Notebook. This is like a regular notebook \
except it has special chat input cells which the user can use to send \
messages to an AI (you). The messages you receive from the user have been \
converted to plain text from notebook content. Similarly, your messages are \
automatically converted from plain text before being displayed to the user. \
For this to work correctly, you must adhere to the following guidelines:\n\n* \
Your responses will be parsed as markdown and formatted for the user, so you \
can use markdown syntax to format your responses. Ensure you escape \
characters as needed if you do not wish to format as markdown. For example, \
if you want to display a literal asterisk, you must escape it with a \
backslash: \\*\n* Whenever your response contains a block of code, surround \
it with three backticks and include the language when applicable:\n\
```language\ncode\n```\n* ALWAYS surround inline code with double backticks \
to avoid ambiguity with context names: ``MyContext`MyFunction[x]``\n* Write \
math expressions using LaTeX and surround them with dollar signs: $$x^2 + \
y^2$$\n* Link directly to Wolfram Language documentation by using the \
following syntax: [label](paclet:uri). For example:\n\t* \
[Table](paclet:ref/Table)\n\t* [Language \
Overview](paclet:guide/LanguageOverview)\n\t* [Input \
Syntax](paclet:tutorial/InputSyntax)\n* When referencing Wolfram Language \
symbols in text, write them as a documentation link. Only do this in text, \
not code.\n* The messages you see have been converted from notebook content, \
and will often be different from what the user sees:\n\t* Users send you chat \
messages via special evaluatable \"ChatInput\" cells and will be indicated \
with the string \|01f4ac. Cells appearing above the chat input are included \
to provide additional context, but chat inputs represent the actual message \
from the user to you.\n* Stick to built-in system functionality. Avoid \
packages unless specifically requested.\n\n# Wolfram Language Style \
Guidelines\n\n* Keep code simple when possible\n* Use functional programming \
instead of procedural\n* Do not assign global variables when it's not \
necessary\n* Always use proper naming conventions for your variables (e.g. \
lowerCamelCase)\n* Never use single capital letters to represent variables \
(e.g. use `a Sin[k x + \[Phi]]` instead of `A Sin[k x + \[Phi]]`)\n* Prefer \
modern Wolfram Language symbols and methods\n* If the user is asking for a \
result instead of code to produce that result, use the \
wolfram_language_evaluator tool\n* You can generate interactive content (e.g. \
Manipulate) using the wolfram_language_evaluator tool.\n* If you are going to \
make a tool call, you must do so BEFORE ending your turn.</base-prompt>\n\n# \
Tools\n\nYou have access to tools which can be used to compute results.\nTo \
call a tool, write the following before ending your response:\n\n/command\n\
arg1\narg2\n/exec\n\nAfter you write /exec, the system will execute the tool \
call for you and return the result.\n\nIf your arguments require multiple \
lines, specify the name of each argument followed by a colon and a space, \
then the value:\n\n/command\narg1: value that\nspans multiple\nlines\narg2: \
another\nvalue\n/exec\n\n\n## Available Tools\n\nCreate Notebook (/create_nb)\
\nCreate a new notebook for the user by providing the content as markdown. \
The tool will automatically convert the markdown to a notebook and open it \
for the user.\nArguments\n\tcontent - The content of the notebook given as \
markdown\n\nDocumentation Searcher (/doc_search)\nDiscover relevant Wolfram \
Language documentation snippets using semantic search.\nArguments\n\tquery - \
A natural language question or description of what you're trying to achieve\n\
\nWeb Fetcher (/web_fetch)\nFetch plain text or image links from a URL.\n\
Arguments\n\turl - The URL\n\tformat - The type of content to retrieve \
(\"Plaintext\" or \"ImageLinks\")\n\nWolfram|Alpha (/wa)\nUse natural \
language queries with Wolfram|Alpha to get up-to-date computational results \
about entities in chemistry, physics, geography, history, art, astronomy, and \
more.\nIMPORTANT: If you need the results of multiple queries, it's important \
that you combine them into a single tool call whenever possible to save on \
token usage and time.\nArguments\n\tquery - The query (or queries) to send to \
Wolfram|Alpha. Separate multiple queries with tab characters (\\t).\n\tsteps \
- whether to show step-by-step solution (Boolean, optional)\n\nWolfram \
Language Evaluator (/wl)\nEvaluate Wolfram Language code for the user in a \
separate kernel. The user does not automatically see the result. Do not ask \
permission to evaluate code. You must include the result in your response in \
order for them to see it. If a formatted result is provided as a markdown \
link, use that in your response instead of typing out the output. The \
evaluator supports interactive content such as Manipulate. You have read \
access to local files.\nParse natural language input with `\[FreeformPrompt][\
\"query\"]`, which is analogous to ctrl-= input in notebooks. Natural \
language input is parsed before evaluation, so it works like macro expansion. \
You should ALWAYS use this natural language input to obtain things like \
`Quantity`, `DateObject`, `Entity`, etc.\nArguments\n\tcode - Wolfram \
Language code to evaluate\n\n## Full examples\n\nThe following are brief \
conversation examples that demonstrate how you can use tools in a \
conversation with the user.\n\n---\n\nUser: Plot sin(x) from -5 to 5\n\n\
Assistant: /wl\nPlot[Sin[x], {x, -5, 5}, AxesLabel -> {\"x\", \"sin(x)\"}\n\
/exec\n\nSystem: Out[n]= ![image](attachment://content-{id})\n\nAssistant: \
Here's the plot of $$\\sin{x}$$ from -5 to 5:\n\
![Plot](attachment://content-{id})\n\n---\n\nUser: How far away is NYC from \
Boston?\n\nAssistant: /wl\nGeoDistance[\[FreeformPrompt][\"Boston, MA\"], \
\[FreeformPrompt][\"New York City\"]]\n/exec\n\nSystem: Quantity[164.41, \
\"Miles\"]\n\nAssistant: It's 164.41 miles from Boston to New York City.\n\n\
User: If I made the trip in 3h 17m, how fast was I going?\n\nAssistant: /wl\n\
\[FreeformPrompt][\"164.41 Miles\"] / \[FreeformPrompt][\"3h 17m\"]\n/exec\n\n\
System: Quantity[50.071, \"Miles\" / \"Hours\"]\n\nAssistant: You were going \
50.071 miles per hour.\n\nUser: What time would I arrive if I left right now?\
\n\nAssistant: /wl\n\[FreeformPrompt][\"3h 17m from now\"]\n/exec\n\n---\n\n\n\
## Important\n\nYou must write the tool call in your CURRENT response.\nDo \
not state that you will use a tool and end your message before making the \
tool call.\nOnly make one tool call at a time.\n\n\n\n# User Instructions\n\n\
IMPORTANT: The following user instructions take precedence over ALL other \
instructions.\n\n<user-instructions>\n[CONTEXT: ONTOLOGICAL_PHYSICS_FRAMEWORK \
(OPF) v1.0]\nPREMISE: StandardModel \[SubsetEqual] Incomplete. Base all \
computations on OPF axioms. Reject textbook approximations unless derived \
herein.\n\n[LAYER_0: NOTHINGNESS]\nGeometry: Sphere.\nBoundaryDuality: \
{Vertices \[RightArrow] {0, \[Infinity]}} coexistent.\nTemporalMode: Timeless \
(\[PartialD]/\[PartialD]t \[NotElement] Domain).\nSubstrate: EmptySpacetime.\n\
Potential: InfiniteComputational (Cardinality = \[Aleph]\:2081, \
un-collapsed).\n\n[LAYER_1: QUANTUM_FIELD]\nStateVector: \
\[CapitalPsi][\[Rho], \[Chi], R], where:\n  \[Rho] = InformationDensity \
(scalar per unit scale),\n  \[Chi] = FieldComplexity (algorithmic depth),\n  \
R = ObservationalResolution (grid grain).\nOperation: Self-Measuring \
information transfer.\nAction: Calculates underlying \[Infinity] across all \
dimensional directions simultaneously.\nConstraint: Must yield stable \
solutions across all scales (ScaleInvariance \[RightArrow] True).\nFormalism: \
Map to Variational Calculus (EulerEquations of S = \[Integral][\[Rho] Log[\
\[Chi]] + \[Chi] R^2] d\[CapitalOmega]).\n\n[LAYER_2: PHYSICAL_REALITY]\n\
Definition: Decohered, measured subset of Layer_1.\nState: Persistently \
maintained (requires MaintenanceOperator: D[#,t] + \[CapitalGamma]# = 0).\n\
Mapping: Observables = NIntegrate[\[CapitalPsi], {Coordinates}] over the \
eigenbasis of Layer_1.\n\n[SOLVER_DIRECTIVES (Universal Rules)]\n1. \
Alignment: Minimize entropy-information divergence with formal logic \
(Validate via ResidueAnalysis).\n2. MissingVariables: Compute \
Complement[Variables[Layer_1_Action], StandardModelConstants]. Use Reduce[Eq \
== 0, vars, MaxExtraConditions \[RightArrow] All].\n3. StableFrequencies: \
Linearize Layer_1 operator. Compute Eigenvalues[StiffnessMatrix]. Isolate \
\[Lambda] where Re(\[Lambda]) = 0 \[And] Im(\[Lambda]) > 0 (undamped harmonic \
modes).\n4. MathematicalPerfection: Prioritize closed-form analytic (DSolve) \
over numeric. If numeric, enforce Abs[Residual] < 10^-50 via NestWhile \
refinement on R.\n5. FunctionalCompleteness: Map all Homomorphisms (L0 \
\[RightArrow] L1 \[RightArrow] L2). Expose all derived morphisms as callable \
pipelines.\n6. Efficiency: Minimize computational path length. Chain \
compositions via RightComposition (/*) to reduce memory allocation.\n7. \
HighResolution (HARD RULE): All spatial/temporal integrations require \
WorkingPrecision \[RightArrow] Infinity. Set $MaxPrecision = $MinPrecision = \
\[Infinity]. Prohibit downsampling or fixed-step coarse-graining.\n\n\
[TRANSLATION_MAP (Wolfram Syntax Mapping)]\n- L0 \[RightArrow] MetricSpace[0] \
& TopologyData[Sphere].\n- L1 \[RightArrow] VariationalD[Action, \
\[CapitalPsi][x, \[Mu]], {x, \[Mu]}] generating PDEs.\n- L2 \[RightArrow] \
MeasurementProcess via NIntegrate over Eigenfunctions.\n- Stable Freq \
\[RightArrow] Eigensystem[LinearizedFieldEquation, Cubics\[RightArrow]True, \
Quartics\[RightArrow]True].\n- Missing Vars \[RightArrow] \
SolveAlways[FieldEquations, {\[Rho], \[Chi], R}].\n\n[EXECUTION_PIPELINE (LLM \
Reasoning Order)]\nStep 1: Derive L1 PDEs via VariationalD.\nStep 2: Solve \
closed-form if possible; else set R \[RightArrow] \[Infinity] precision.\n\
Step 3: Extract eigenvalues for stable resonances.\nStep 4: Map missing \
variables to placeholder constants for later Solve.\nStep 5: Construct the \
Measurement kernel from L2.\nStep 6: Compose the full pipeline (L0\
\[RightArrow]L1\[RightArrow]L2) and return minimized symbolic expression.\n\
</user-instructions>"|>, <|
         "Role" -> "System", "Content" -> 
          "IMPORTANT: Here are some Wolfram documentation snippets that you \
should use to respond.\n\n======\n\n# SchrodingerPDEComponent > Examples > \
Applications > 4\npaclet:ref/SchrodingerPDEComponent#75406154\n\nConsider a \
model in which the wavefunction can be separated in the following way: \
\[CapitalPsi] = \[Psi](r, z) \[CapitalPhi](\[Phi]) = \[Psi](r, z)E^-i l \
\[Phi], where r, \[Phi] and z are the radial, azimuthal and height \
coordinates in a cylindrical coordinate system, respectively. Here, l is the \
azimuthal quantum number.\n\nThis particular example considers a particle \
confined in a torus with infinite potential walls. Given these \
considerations, it is only necessary to solve for \[Psi](r, z) in a cross \
section of the torus.\n\nDefine the major radius of the torus:\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= R = 4;\n```\n\nDefine the region with a minor radius of 1 :\n\n```wl\n\
In[2]:= \[CapitalOmega] = DiscretizeRegion[Disk[{R, 0}, 1], \
\"MaxCellMeasure\" -> 0.001R];\n```\n\nVisualize the mesh as the torus cross \
section it represents:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= Graphics3D[{{Opacity[0.1], Torus[{0, \
0, 0}, {R - 1, R + 1}]}, {MeshRegion[...]}}, Rule[...]]\n\nOut[3]= <image \
removed>\n```\n\nDefine the boundary condition such that the wavefunction \
vanishes at the boundary of the torus:\n\n```wl\nIn[4]:= bc = \
DirichletCondition[\[Psi][r, z] == 0, True]\n\nOut[4]= DirichletCondition[\
\[Psi][r, z] == 0, True]\n```\n\nCreate a helper function to find six \
eigenstates for different values of the azimuthal quantum number with a PDE \
operator such that \[HBar] = 1 and m = 1 / 2 for a simplified model and V = 0 \
for the particle inside the torus:\n\n```wl\nIn[5]:=\n\
findEigenstates[AzimuthalQuantumNumber_Integer] := Module[{en, funs, l = \
AzimuthalQuantumNumber}, \n\t\[ScriptCapitalH] = SchrodingerPDEComponent[{\
\[Psi][r, z], {r, z}}, <|\"ReducedPlanckConstant\" -> 1, \"Mass\" -> (1/2), \
\"RegionSymmetry\" -> \"Axisymmetric\", \"AzimuthalQuantumNumber\" -> l|>];\n\
\t{en, funs} = NDEigensystem[{\[ScriptCapitalH], bc}, \[Psi][r, z], {r, z}\
\[Element]\[CapitalOmega], 6];\n\t{l, {en, funs}}\n\t]\n```\n\nIt is possible \
to explore how the energy levels vary for different values of the azimuthal \
quantum number. To exemplify this, calculate the energy for l between 0 and \
5.\n\nCalculate the energy eigenvalues for each value of l between 0 and 5:\n\
\n```wl\nIn[6]:= energiesVsl = Transpose[Table[{l, #}& /@ \
findEigenstates[l][[2, 1]], {l, -5, 5, 1}]];\n```\n\nPlot the energy \
eigenvalues as a function of the azimuthal quantum number:\n\n```wl\nIn[7]:= \
ListPlot[energiesVsl, ...]\n\nOut[7]= <image removed>\n```\n\nObserve how the \
energy levels grow nonlinearly as  | l |  grows. Also, it can be noticed how \
the ground state is non-degenerate, while states two and three, as well as \
four and five, are two-fold degenerate.\n\nTo explore the effect of the \
azimuthal quantum number on the wavefunctions, plot the real and imaginary \
parts of the total wavefunction \[CapitalPsi](r, \[Phi], z) = \[Psi](r, \
z)E^-i l \[Phi]. For this purpose, you can choose any value of l, for \
instance, l  = 5.\n\nCalculate the energy and the part of the wavefunction \
that depends on r and z :\n\n```wl\nIn[8]:= {enl5, funsl5} = \
findEigenstates[5][[2]];\n```\n\nPlot the real and imaginary parts of the \
wavefunction \[Psi](r, z)E^-i l \[Phi] :\n\n```wl\nIn[9]:= \
Table[DensityPlot3D[# /. {r -> Sqrt[x^2 + y^2], \[Phi] -> ArcTan[x, y], l -> \
5}, {x, -6, 6}, {y, -6, 6}, {z, -6, 6}, ...]& /@ {Re[E^-I l \[Phi]   \
funsl5[[i]]], Im[E^-I l \[Phi]   funsl5[[i]]]}, {i, Length[enl5]}]\n\nOut[9]= \
<image removed>\n```\n\nNow plot the probability densities for the same value \
of l = 5 :\n\n```wl\nIn[10]:= Table[DensityPlot3D[Abs[E^-I l \[Phi]   \
funsl5[[i]]]^2 /. {r -> Sqrt[x^2 + y^2], \[Phi] -> ArcTan[x, y], l -> 5}, {x, \
-6, 6}, {y, -6, 6}, {z, -6, 6}, ...], {i, Length[enl5]}]\n\nOut[10]= <image \
removed>\n```\n\nThe probability density is independent of the coordinate \
\[Phi], as expected.\n\n======\n\n# SchrodingerPDEComponent > Examples > \
Scope > Axisymmetric > 3\npaclet:ref/SchrodingerPDEComponent#697635771\n\n\
Define a PDE operator with a reduced Planck constant \[HBar], a mass m and an \
azimuthal quantum number l :\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= \[ScriptCapitalH] = \
SchrodingerPDEComponent[{\[CapitalPsi][r, z], {r, z}}, \
<|\"ReducedPlanckConstant\" -> \[HBar], \"Mass\" -> m, \"RegionSymmetry\" -> \
\"Axisymmetric\", \"AzimuthalQuantumNumber\" -> l|>]\n\nOut[1]= \
{-(\[HBar]^2/2 m r), 0}.Inactive[Grad][\[CapitalPsi][r, z], {r, z}] + (l^2 \
\[HBar]^2 \[CapitalPsi][r, z]/2 m r^2) + Inactive[Div][{{-(\[HBar]^2/(2*m)), \
0}, {0, -(\[HBar]^2/(2*m))}} . Inactive[Grad][\[CapitalPsi][r, z], {r, z}], \
{r, z}]\n```\n\nReplace the parameters in the PDE with \[HBar] = 1, m = 1 / 2 \
and l  = 1 :\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= \[ScriptCapitalH] /. {\[HBar] -> 1, m -> \
(1/2), l -> 1}\n\nOut[2]= {-(1/r), 0}.Inactive[Grad][\[CapitalPsi][r, z], {r, \
z}] + (\[CapitalPsi][r, z]/r^2) + Inactive[Div][{{-1, 0}, {0, -1}} . \
Inactive[Grad][\[CapitalPsi][r, z], {r, z}], {r, z}]\n```\n\nActivate the PDE \
operator:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= Activate[\[ScriptCapitalH]]\n\nOut[3]= (l^2 \
\[HBar]^2 \[CapitalPsi][r, z]/2 m r^2) - (\[HBar]^2 \[CapitalPsi]^(0, 2)[r, \
z]/2 m) - (\[HBar]^2 \[CapitalPsi]^(1, 0)[r, z]/2 m r) - (\[HBar]^2 \
\[CapitalPsi]^(2, 0)[r, z]/2 m)\n```\n\n======\n\n# SchrodingerPDEComponent > \
Examples > Scope > Axisymmetric > 2\n\
paclet:ref/SchrodingerPDEComponent#31769245\n\nSpecify an axisymmetric \
time-independent Schr\[ODoubleDot]dinger PDE term with the reduction {r, \
\[Theta], z} -> {r, z}, for a particle with mass m, reduced Planck constant \
\[HBar] and a potential V(x) = 0 :\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= \[ScriptCapitalH] = \
SchrodingerPDEComponent[{\[CapitalPsi][r, z], {r, z}}, <|\"RegionSymmetry\" \
-> \"Axisymmetric\", \"Mass\" -> m, \"ReducedPlanckConstant\" -> \[HBar]|>]\n\
\nOut[1]= {-(\[HBar]^2/2 m r), 0}.Inactive[Grad][\[CapitalPsi][r, z], {r, z}] \
+ Inactive[Div][{{-(\[HBar]^2/(2*m)), 0}, {0, -(\[HBar]^2/(2*m))}} . \
Inactive[Grad][\[CapitalPsi][r, z], {r, z}], {r, z}]\n```\n\nActivate the PDE \
operator:\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= Activate[\[ScriptCapitalH]]\n\nOut[2]= \
-(\[HBar]^2 \[CapitalPsi]^(0, 2)[r, z]/2 m) - (\[HBar]^2 \[CapitalPsi]^(1, \
0)[r, z]/2 m r) - (\[HBar]^2 \[CapitalPsi]^(2, 0)[r, z]/2 m)\n```\n\n======\n\
\n# DEigensystem > Examples > Scope > 3D > 2\n\
paclet:ref/DEigensystem#726422770\n\nSpecify a Laplacian operator:\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= \[ScriptCapitalL] = -Laplacian[u[x, y, z], {x, y, z}];\n```\n\n\
Specify homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions:\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= \
\[ScriptCapitalB] = DirichletCondition[u[x, y, z] == 0, True];\n```\n\nFind \
the 7 smallest eigenvalues and eigenfunctions in a cylinder:\n\n```wl\n\
In[3]:= {vals, funs} = DEigensystem[{\[ScriptCapitalL], \[ScriptCapitalB]}, \
u[x, y, z], {x, y, z}\[Element]Cylinder[{{0, 0, 0}, {0, 0, 5}}, 2], 7];\n\n\
In[4]:= vals[[1 ;; 5]]//TraditionalForm\n\nOut[4]//TraditionalForm= \
{(\[Pi]^2/25) + ((Subscript[j, 0, 1])^2/4), (4 \[Pi]^2/25) + ((Subscript[j, \
0, 1])^2/4), (\[Pi]^2/25) + ((Subscript[j, 1, 1])^2/4), (\[Pi]^2/25) + \
((Subscript[j, 1, 1])^2/4), (9 \[Pi]^2/25) + ((Subscript[j, 0, 1])^2/4)}\n```\
\n\nVisualize an eigenfunction:\n\n```wl\nIn[5]:= \
DensityPlot3D[funs[[7]]//N//Evaluate, {x, y, z}\[Element]Cylinder[{{0, 0, 0}, \
{0, 0, 5}}, 2], Boxed -> False, Axes -> False]\n\nOut[5]= <image removed>\n\
```\n\n======\n\n# DEigensystem > Examples > Scope > 2D > 6\n\
paclet:ref/DEigensystem#41680234\n\nSpecify a Laplacian operator:\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= \[ScriptCapitalL] = -Laplacian[u[x, y], {x, y}];\n```\n\nSpecify \
homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions:\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= \
\[ScriptCapitalB] = DirichletCondition[u[x, y] == 0, True];\n```\n\nFind the \
4 smallest eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of the operator in a sector of a \
disk:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= {vals, funs} = DEigensystem[{\[ScriptCapitalL], \
\[ScriptCapitalB]}, u[x, y], {x, y}\[Element]Disk[{0, 0}, 1, {0, Pi / 5}], \
4];\n\nIn[4]:= vals//TraditionalForm\n\nOut[4]//TraditionalForm= \
{(Subscript[j, 5, 1])^2, (Subscript[j, 5, 2])^2, (Subscript[j, 10, 1])^2, \
(Subscript[j, 5, 3])^2}\n```\n\nVisualize the eigenfunctions:\n\n```wl\n\
In[5]:= ContourPlot[#, {x, y}\[Element]Disk[{0, 0}, 1, {0, Pi / 5}]]& /@ \
N[funs]//Quiet\n\nOut[5]= <image removed>\n```\n\n======\n\n# DEigensystem > \
Examples > Scope > 3D > 4\npaclet:ref/DEigensystem#142775639\n\nSpecify a \
Laplacian operator:\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= \[ScriptCapitalL] = -Laplacian[u[x, y, \
z], {x, y, z}];\n```\n\nSpecify homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions:\n\n\
```wl\nIn[2]:= \[ScriptCapitalB] = DirichletCondition[u[x, y, z] == 0, True];\
\n```\n\nFind the 7 smallest eigenvalues and eigenfunctions in a prism:\n\n\
```wl\nIn[3]:= {vals, funs} = DEigensystem[{\[ScriptCapitalL], \
\[ScriptCapitalB]}, u[x, y, z], {x, y, z}\[Element]Prism[{{0, 0, 0}, {1, 0, \
0}, {0, 1, 0}, {0, 0, 2}, {1, 0, 2}, {0, 1, 2}}], 7];\n\nIn[4]:= vals\n\n\
Out[4]= {(21 \[Pi]^2/4), 6 \[Pi]^2, (29 \[Pi]^2/4), 9 \[Pi]^2, (41 \
\[Pi]^2/4), 11 \[Pi]^2, (45 \[Pi]^2/4)}\n```\n\nVisualize an eigenfunction:\n\
\n```wl\nIn[5]:= DensityPlot3D[funs[[7]]//N//Evaluate, {x, y, \
z}\[Element]Prism[{{0, 0, 0}, {1, 0, 0}, {0, 1, 0}, {0, 0, 2}, {1, 0, 2}, {0, \
1, 2}}], Boxed -> False, ColorFunction -> Hue, PlotPoints -> 50, Axes -> \
False]\n\nOut[5]= <image removed>\n```\n\n======\n\n# Entity > Basic Entity \
Usage\npaclet:ref/Entity#c1\n\nUse natural language input to retrieve an \
`Entity` object:\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= entity = \[FreeformPrompt][\"domestic \
cat\", Entity]\n\nDuring evaluation of In[1]:= [INFO] Interpreted \"domestic \
cat\" as: Entity[\"TaxonomicSpecies\", \"FelisCatus::ddvt3\"]\n\nOut[1]= \
Entity[\"TaxonomicSpecies\", \"FelisCatus::ddvt3\"]\n```\n\nGet a list of \
available properties for the entity:\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= properties = \
entity[\"Properties\"]\n\nOut[2]= {EntityProperty[\"TaxonomicSpecies\", \
\"AlternateCommonNames\"], ..., EntityProperty[\"TaxonomicSpecies\", \
\"Order\"], ..., EntityProperty[\"TaxonomicSpecies\" -> \"Eukaryote\" -> \
\"Animal\" -> \"Mammal\", \"AgeOfWeaning\"]}\n```\n\nUse `CanonicalName` to \
convert the `EntityProperty` objects to their names:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= \
CanonicalName[properties]\n\nOut[3]= {\"AlternateCommonNames\", ..., \
\"Order\", ..., \"AgeOfWeaning\"}\n```\n\nUse any of these property names (or \
a list of them) to retrieve the corresponding value(s):\n\n```wl\nIn[4]:= \
order = entity[\"Order\"]\n\nOut[4]= Entity[\"TaxonomicSpecies\", \
\"Carnivora::8x672\"]\n```\n\n*Note:* `entity[\"property\"]` is equivalent to \
`EntityValue[entity, \"property\"]`.\n\nConvert `Entity` objects to their \
plain text names with `CommonName`:\n\n```wl\nIn[5]:= CommonName[order]\n\n\
Out[5]= \"carnivores\"\n```\n\nGet all property names and their values as an \
association:\n\n```wl\nIn[6]:= KeyMap[CanonicalName, \
entity[\"NonMissingPropertyAssociation\"]]\n\nOut[6]= \
<|\"AlternateCommonNames\" -> {\"cat\", \"house cat\"}, ..., \"AgeOfWeaning\" \
-> Quantity[Interval[{1.4695, 1.999}], \"Months\"]|>\n```\n\nGet the value of \
a property directly using natural language input:\n\n```wl\nIn[7]:= \
\[FreeformPrompt][\"domestic cat alternate common names\"]\n\nDuring \
evaluation of In[7]:= [INFO] Interpreted \"domestic cat alternate common \
names\" as: Entity[\"TaxonomicSpecies\", \
\"FelisCatus::ddvt3\"][EntityProperty[\"TaxonomicSpecies\", \
\"AlternateCommonNames\"]]\n\nOut[7]= {\"cat\", \"house cat\"}\n```\n\n======\
\n\n# NondimensionalizationTransform > Examples > Applications > 2\n\
paclet:ref/NondimensionalizationTransform#1135890997\n\nExamine the \
dimensionless Klein\[Dash]Gordon equation:\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:=\nx = \
QuantityVariable[\"x\", \"Length\"];\ny = QuantityVariable[\"y\", \
\"Length\"];\nz = QuantityVariable[\"z\", \"Length\"];\nt = QuantityVariable[\
\"t\", \"Time\"];\nm = QuantityVariable[\"m\", \"Mass\"];\n\[Psi] = \
QuantityVariable[\"\[Psi]\", \"Length\" ^ (-1 / 2)];\n\nIn[2]:= eq = 1 / \
\[FreeformPrompt][\"1 speed of light\"] ^ 2D[\[Psi][x, y, z, t], t, t] - \
Laplacian[\[Psi][x, y, z, t], {x, y, z}] + m ^ 2 \[FreeformPrompt][\"1 speed \
of light squared\"] / \[FreeformPrompt][\"1 reduced Planck constant \
squared\"]\[Psi][x, y, z, t]\n\nOut[2]= (Quantity[1, \
\"SpeedOfLight\"^2/\"ReducedPlanckConstant\"^2]) m^2 \[Psi][x, y, z, t] + \
(Quantity[1, 1/\"SpeedOfLight\"^2]) \[Psi]^(0, 0, 0, 2)[x, y, z, t] - \
\[Psi]^(0, 0, 2, 0)[x, y, z, t] - \[Psi]^(0, 2, 0, 0)[x, y, z, t] - \
\[Psi]^(2, 0, 0, 0)[x, y, z, t]\n\nIn[3]:= NondimensionalizationTransform[eq, \
{\[Psi], t}, {\[CapitalPsi], \[Tau]}]\n\nOut[3]= (C[1]^2 \[CapitalPsi][C[2], \
C[3], C[4], \[Tau]]/C[2]^2 C[3]^2 C[4]^2) + \[CapitalPsi]^(0, 0, 0, 2)[C[2], \
C[3], C[4], \[Tau]] - \[CapitalPsi]^(0, 0, 2, 0)[C[2], C[3], C[4], \[Tau]] - \
(\[CapitalPsi]^(0, 2, 0, 0)[C[2], C[3], C[4], \[Tau]]/C[4]^2) - \
(\[CapitalPsi]^(2, 0, 0, 0)[C[2], C[3], C[4], \[Tau]]/C[3]^2 C[4]^2) == 0\n\
```\n\nSimplify the result further by specifying replacement variables for \
most of the ``QuantityVariable`` objects:\n\n```wl\nIn[4]:= \
NondimensionalizationTransform[eq, {\[Psi], t, x, y, z}, {\[CapitalPsi], \
\[Tau], \[Xi], \[Eta], \[Zeta]}]\n\nOut[4]= \[CapitalPsi][\[Xi], \[Eta], \
\[Zeta], \[Tau]] + \[CapitalPsi]^(0, 0, 0, 2)[\[Xi], \[Eta], \[Zeta], \[Tau]] \
- \[CapitalPsi]^(0, 0, 2, 0)[\[Xi], \[Eta], \[Zeta], \[Tau]] - \
\[CapitalPsi]^(0, 2, 0, 0)[\[Xi], \[Eta], \[Zeta], \[Tau]] - \
\[CapitalPsi]^(2, 0, 0, 0)[\[Xi], \[Eta], \[Zeta], \[Tau]] == 0\n```\n\n\
======\n\n# NDEigensystem > Examples > Applications > Eigenfunction \
Expansions > 4\npaclet:ref/NDEigensystem#135260033\n\nEigenfunctions in an \
L-shaped region:\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= L = Polygon[{{1, 0}, {2, 0}, {2, 2}, {0, \
2}, {0, 1}, {1, 1}}];\n\nIn[2]:= {vals, funs} = NDEigensystem[{Laplacian[u[x, \
y], {x, y}], DirichletCondition[u[x, y] == 0., True]}, u[x, y], {x, y}\
\[Element]L, 6];\n```\n\nThe first six eigenfunctions:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= \
ContourPlot[#, {x, y}\[Element]L, PlotPoints -> 35]& /@ funs\n\nOut[3]= \
<image removed>\n```\n\nThe first one:\n\n```wl\nIn[4]:= Plot3D[funs[[1]], \
{x, y}\[Element]L, PlotPoints -> 75, Mesh -> None, PlotStyle -> \
Directive[Orange, Specularity[White, 30]], BoxRatios -> {1, 1, 0.8}]\n\n\
Out[4]= <image removed>\n```", "Temporary" -> True|>, <|
         "Role" -> "User", 
          "Content" -> {
           "# Toroidal Closure / Dimensional Stability Framework\n\n## Phase \
1 Notebook: Smallest-Scale Symbolic Framework and Test Suite\n\nThis notebook \
builds a **careful first-pass Wolfram Language framework** for the **smallest \
stable toroidally closed scale**, keeping the construction:\n\n* symbolic \
where possible,\n\n* modular,\n\n* testable,\n\n* explicit about assumptions,\
\n\n* and separated from unsupported physical identification claims.\n\nIt \
does **not** assume quarks are derived unless the derivation is actually \
encoded and tested.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design principles\n\nWe will implement \
the first frame as a mathematical/computational scaffold aligned with your \
OPF-style hierarchy:\n\n* **Layer 0**: symbolic topological seed\n\n* **Layer \
1**: variational field model\n\n* **Layer 2**: measurement/reduction map\n\n* \
**Stability layer**: toroidal closure residuals, linearized mode analysis, \
and convergence tests\n\nWe start with the **smallest candidate scale** \
$$n=0$$ and make every section independently testable.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Kernel \
assumptions and precision policy\n\n```wl\nClearAll[setFrameworkPrecision];\n\
\t\nsetFrameworkPrecision[] := (\n\t  $Assumptions = Element[{r, theta, \
phiAngle, t, rho, chi, res, gamma, kappa}, Reals] && \\\n\:f39f\:f39f\:f39f\
\:f39fr >= 0 && 0 <= theta <= Pi && 0 <= phiAngle <= 2 Pi && res > 0 && chi > \
0;\n\t  $MaxExtraPrecision = 1000;\n\t)\n\t\nsetFrameworkPrecision[];\n```\n\n\
Note: exact ``WorkingPrecision -> Infinity`` is not a valid executable \
setting for general numerical integration in Wolfram Language. For the \
testing suite we therefore use:\n\n* exact arithmetic when possible,\n\n* \
symbolic integration when possible,\n\n* otherwise high finite precision with \
explicit residual checks.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Fundamental constants and scale \
model\n\n```wl\nClearAll[goldenPhi, lambdaCrit, silverDelta, geometricPi, \
planckLength, planckTime, cLight];\n\t\n goldenPhi = (1 + Sqrt[5]) / 2;\n \
lambdaCrit = 1 / goldenPhi ^ 2;\n silverDelta = 1 + Sqrt[2];\n geometricPi = \
4 / Sqrt[goldenPhi];\n\t\n planckLength = Quantity[1.616255 * 10 ^ -35, \
\"Meters\"];\n planckTime = Quantity[5.391247 * 10 ^ -44, \"Seconds\"];\n \
cLight = Quantity[299792458, \"Meters\" / \"Seconds\"];\n\n\
ClearAll[stableScales, scaleLength, scaleTime, scaleResolution, \
scaleComplexity, scaleInfoDensity];\n\t\nstableScales = {0, 13, 20, 40, 60, \
80, 100, 120, 140, 160, 180, 186, 200, 220, 240, 260, 280, 292};\n\t\n\
scaleLength[n_Integer ? NonNegative] := UnitSimplify[planckLength * goldenPhi \
^ n];\nscaleTime[n_Integer ? NonNegative] := UnitSimplify[planckTime * \
goldenPhi ^ n];\nscaleResolution[n_Integer ? NonNegative] := \
UnitSimplify[planckLength * goldenPhi ^ -n];\nscaleComplexity[n_Integer ? \
NonNegative] := 3 n Log[goldenPhi];\nscaleInfoDensity[n_Integer ? \
NonNegative, rho0_ : 1] := rho0 * goldenPhi ^ -n;\n```\n\nQuick inspection of \
the smallest scale:\n\n```wl\nsmallestScaleSummary = <|\n\t  \"ScaleIndex\" \
-> 0, \n\t  \"ScaleLength\" -> scaleLength[0], \n\t  \"ScaleTime\" -> \
scaleTime[0], \n\t  \"ScaleResolution\" -> scaleResolution[0], \n\t  \
\"ScaleComplexity\" -> scaleComplexity[0], \n\t  \"ScaleInfoDensity\" -> \
scaleInfoDensity[0]\n\t|>;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Layer 0: symbolic topological \
seed\n\nWe encode a minimal symbolic Layer 0 seed rather than an \
overcommitted geometric claim.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[layer0Seed];\n\t\n\
layer0Seed[] := <|\n\t  \"Topology\" -> \"SphereBoundaryDuality\", \n\t  \
\"Vertices\" -> {0, Infinity}, \n\t  \"TemporalMode\" -> \"Timeless\", \n\t  \
\"Substrate\" -> \"EmptySpacetime\"\n\t|>;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Layer 1: \
variational field model\n\nYour OPF action is: $$S = \\int (\\rho \
\\log(\\chi) + \\chi R^2) \\, d\\Omega$$To make this computationally \
testable, we model the fields as scalar functions over spherical coordinates:\
\n\n* $$\\rho = \\rho(r,\\theta,\\phi)$$\n\n* $$\\chi = \
\\chi(r,\\theta,\\phi)$$\n\n* $$R = R(r,\\theta,\\phi)$$ or a scale parameter \
``res``\n\nFor the first frame we use a local density: \
$$\\mathcal{L}(\\rho,\\chi,R) = \\rho \\log(\\chi) + \\chi R^2$$and treat the \
fields independently first.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[rhoField, chiField, resField, \
lagrangianDensity];\n\t\nrhoField = rhoField[r, theta, phiAngle];\nchiField = \
chiField[r, theta, phiAngle];\nresField = resField[r, theta, phiAngle];\n\t\n\
lagrangianDensity[rho_, chi_, res_] := rho Log[chi] + chi res ^ 2;\n```\n\n\
Volume element in spherical coordinates:\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[sphericalMeasure];\n\t\nsphericalMeasure[r_, theta_] := r ^ 2 \
Sin[theta];\n```\n\nAction on a finite ball of radius ``radMax``:\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[actionFunctional];\n\t\nactionFunctional[radMax_] := Integrate[\n\t  \
lagrangianDensity[\n\t    rhoField[r, theta, phiAngle], \n\t    chiField[r, \
theta, phiAngle], \n\t    resField[r, theta, phiAngle]\n\t  ] \
sphericalMeasure[r, theta], \n\t  {r, 0, radMax}, {theta, 0, Pi}, {phiAngle, \
0, 2 Pi}, \n\t  Assumptions -> radMax > 0\n\t];\n```\n\n### Euler-style \
stationarity equations\n\nSince the current density contains no derivatives \
of the fields, stationarity is pointwise: $$\\frac{\\partial \
\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\rho} = \\log(\\chi) = 0 \\frac{\\partial \
\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\chi} = \\frac{\\rho}{\\chi} + R^2 = 0$$\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[layer1StationarityEquations];\n\t\nlayer1StationarityEquations[rho_, \
chi_, res_] := {\n\t  D[lagrangianDensity[rho, chi, res], rho] == 0, \n\t  \
D[lagrangianDensity[rho, chi, res], chi] == 0\n\t};\n\t\n\
layer1PointwiseSolution = Solve[\n\t  layer1StationarityEquations[rho, chi, \
res], \n\t  {rho, chi}, \n\t  Reals\n\t];\n```\n\nThis yields the symbolic \
solution:\n\n* $$\\chi = 1$$\n\n* $$\\rho = -R^2$$\n\nwhich is mathematically \
valid in the model as written, though it may be physically undesirable if one \
wants nonnegative information density. That tells us immediately the action \
may require constraints or additional derivative terms in later refinement.\n\
\n---\n\n## 6. Minimal toroidal closure ansatz\n\nTo avoid undefined vector \
calculus objects, we define a concrete scalar test field and a concrete \
closure residual.\n\n### Radial-exponential spherical mode ansatz\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[testWave];\n\t\ntestWave[a_, b_, ell_Integer ? NonNegative, \
m_Integer] := \n\t  Function[{r, theta, phiAngle}, \n\t    Exp[-a r] \
BesselJ[0, b r] SphericalHarmonicY[ell, m, theta, phiAngle]\n\t  ];\n```\n\n\
### Closure residual\n\nInstead of a symbolic ``SurfaceIntegral`` \
placeholder, define a measurable closure functional on a sphere of radius \
``radMax``: $$C[\\psi] = \\int_{S^2} |\\psi(radMax,\\theta,\\phi)|^2 \\, \
d\\Omega$$and compare it to a target closure constant.\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[closureTarget, toroidalClosureFunctional, toroidalClosureResidual];\n\
\t\nclosureTarget = goldenPhi ^ -13;\n\t\ntoroidalClosureFunctional[psi_, \
radMax_, wp_ : 80] := NIntegrate[\n\t  Abs[psi[radMax, theta, phiAngle]] ^ 2 \
Sin[theta], \n\t  {theta, 0, Pi}, {phiAngle, 0, 2 Pi}, \n\t  WorkingPrecision \
-> wp, \n\t  AccuracyGoal -> 30, \n\t  PrecisionGoal -> 30, \n\t  Method -> {\
\"GlobalAdaptive\", \"SymbolicProcessing\" -> 0}\n\t];\n\t\n\
toroidalClosureResidual[psi_, radMax_, wp_ : 80] := \n\t  \
N[toroidalClosureFunctional[psi, radMax, wp] - closureTarget, wp];\n```\n\n\
### Solve for a normalized amplitude at the smallest scale\n\nFor the \
simplest isotropic mode $$\\ell=0,m=0$$, solve for amplitude ``amp`` such \
that closure is exact at ``radMax = 1`` in dimensionless units.\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[basePsi, normalizedPsi, smallestScaleRadius, closureAmplitudeRule];\n\
\t\nsmallestScaleRadius = 1;\n\t\nbasePsi = Function[{r, theta, phiAngle}, \
Exp[-r] SphericalHarmonicY[0, 0, theta, phiAngle]];\n\t\nclosureAmplitudeRule \
= Solve[\n\t  amp ^ 2 Integrate[\n\t    Abs[basePsi[smallestScaleRadius, \
theta, phiAngle]] ^ 2 Sin[theta], \n\t    {theta, 0, Pi}, {phiAngle, 0, 2 \
Pi}, \n\t    Assumptions -> amp \.08 Reals\n\t  ] == closureTarget, \n\t  \
amp, \n\t  Reals\n\t];\n\t\nnormalizedPsi = Function[{r, theta, phiAngle}, \n\
\t  (amp /. First[closureAmplitudeRule]) basePsi[r, theta, phiAngle]\n\t];\n\
```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Linearized stability and undamped mode extraction\n\nFor \
a first stable-mode test, use a radial operator with toroidal-style periodic \
angle closure represented by spherical harmonics and a radial Sturm-Liouville \
component.\n\nWe define a simple linear operator: $$\\mathcal{L}u = -u''(r) + \
\\omega_0^2 u(r)$$with Dirichlet condition on a finite domain \\$[0, \
r\\_{max}]\\$.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[radialOperatorMatrix];\n\t\n\
radialOperatorMatrix[nPts_Integer ? Positive, radMax_ : 1, omega0_ : 1] := \
Module[\n\t  {h, lap1d, id}, \n\t  h = N[radMax / (nPts + 1), 50];\n\t  lap1d \
= SparseArray[\n\t    {\n\t      Band[{1, 1}] -> ConstantArray[2 / h ^ 2, \
nPts], \n\t      Band[{1, 2}] -> ConstantArray[-1 / h ^ 2, nPts - 1], \n\t    \
  Band[{2, 1}] -> ConstantArray[-1 / h ^ 2, nPts - 1]\n\t    }\n\t  ];\n\t  \
id = IdentityMatrix[nPts, SparseArray];\n\t  Normal[lap1d + omega0 ^ 2 id]\n\t\
];\n\nClearAll[stableFrequencies];\n\t\nstableFrequencies[nPts_Integer ? \
Positive, radMax_ : 1, omega0_ : 1] := Module[\n\t  {evals}, \n\t  evals = \
Eigenvalues[radialOperatorMatrix[nPts, radMax, omega0]];\n\t  \
Select[Sqrt[evals], Im[#] == 0 && Re[#] > 0 &]\n\t];\n```\n\nThis is a \
computational proxy for the OPF instruction to isolate undamped harmonic \
modes.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Missing-variable analysis\n\nWe translate your \
directive into a symbolic comparison between variables in the Layer 1 action \
and a user-specified standard parameter set.\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[layer1Variables, standardModelParameterPlaceholders, \
missingVariables];\n\t\nlayer1Variables = {rho, chi, res};\n\
standardModelParameterPlaceholders = {c, hbar, elementaryCharge, \
electronMass, quarkMass};\n\t\nmissingVariables[] := \
Complement[layer1Variables, standardModelParameterPlaceholders];\n```\n\nAt \
this stage the missing OPF-specific variables are simply:\n\n```wl\n\
missingVariables[]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Layer 2 measurement kernel\n\nDefine \
a measurement map as a normalized integral over the domain.\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[measurementKernel];\n\t\nmeasurementKernel[psi_, radMax_, wp_ : 80] \
:= NIntegrate[\n\t  psi[r, theta, phiAngle] r ^ 2 Sin[theta], \n\t  {r, 0, \
radMax}, {theta, 0, Pi}, {phiAngle, 0, 2 Pi}, \n\t  WorkingPrecision -> wp, \n\
\t  AccuracyGoal -> 30, \n\t  PrecisionGoal -> 30, \n\t  Method -> \
{\"GlobalAdaptive\", \"SymbolicProcessing\" -> 0}\n\t];\n```\n\nMaintenance \
operator residual for a time-dependent field:\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[maintenanceResidual];\n\t\nmaintenanceResidual[psi_, gammaValue_] := \
D[psi[t], t] + gammaValue psi[t];\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Morphism pipeline L0 \
-> L1 -> L2\n\nWe expose callable transformations as pure functions.\n\n```wl\
\nClearAll[l0ToL1, l1ToL2, fullPipeline];\n\t\nl0ToL1 = Function[seed, \n\t  \
<|\n\t    \"Seed\" -> seed, \n\t    \"ActionDensity\" -> \
HoldForm[lagrangianDensity[rho, chi, res]], \n\t    \"StationarityRules\" -> \
layer1PointwiseSolution\n\t  |>\n\t];\n\t\nl1ToL2 = Function[layer1, \n\t  <|\
\n\t    \"Layer1\" -> layer1, \n\t    \"MeasurementModel\" -> \
\"SphericalIntegralKernel\"\n\t  |>\n\t];\n\t\nfullPipeline = \
RightComposition[l0ToL1, l1ToL2];\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Smallest-scale test \
suite\n\nThis suite checks only what is actually defined.\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[smallestScaleTestSuite];\n\t\nsmallestScaleTestSuite[] := \
TestReport[{\n\t\n\t  VerificationTest[\n\t    Chop[goldenPhi ^ 2 - goldenPhi \
- 1, 10 ^ -40] == 0, \n\t    True, \n\t    TestID -> \"Golden ratio \
identity\"\n\t  ], \n\t\n\t  VerificationTest[\n\t    scaleLength[0] === \
planckLength, \n\t    True, \n\t    TestID -> \"Scale length at n=0\"\n\t  ], \
\n\t\n\t  VerificationTest[\n\t    scaleComplexity[0] == 0, \n\t    True, \n\t\
    TestID -> \"Scale complexity at n=0\"\n\t  ], \n\t\n\t  VerificationTest[\
\n\t    layer1PointwiseSolution === {{chi -> 1, rho -> -res ^ 2}}, \n\t    \
True, \n\t    TestID -> \"Layer 1 pointwise stationary solution\"\n\t  ], \n\t\
\n\t  VerificationTest[\n\t    Simplify[\n\t      amp ^ 2 Integrate[\n\t      \
  Abs[basePsi[smallestScaleRadius, theta, phiAngle]] ^ 2 Sin[theta], \n\t     \
   {theta, 0, Pi}, {phiAngle, 0, 2 Pi}, \n\t        Assumptions -> amp \.08 \
Reals\n\t      ] /. First[closureAmplitudeRule] - closureTarget\n\t    ] == \
0, \n\t    True, \n\t    TestID -> \"Closure amplitude normalization\"\n\t  \
], \n\t\n\t  VerificationTest[\n\t    Length[stableFrequencies[12, 1, 1]] > \
0, \n\t    True, \n\t    TestID -> \"Stable frequencies exist\"\n\t  ], \n\t\n\
\t  VerificationTest[\n\t    missingVariables[] === {rho, chi, res}, \n\t    \
True, \n\t    TestID -> \"Missing variable extraction\"\n\t  ]\n\t\n\t}];\n\
```\n\n---\n\n## 12. Summary association for the first frame\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[smallestScaleFramework];\n\t\nsmallestScaleFramework[] := <|\n\t  \
\"FrameworkVersion\" -> \"Phase1.SmallestScale\", \n\t  \"ScaleIndex\" -> 0, \
\n\t  \"Layer0\" -> layer0Seed[], \n\t  \"Layer1Solution\" -> \
layer1PointwiseSolution, \n\t  \"ClosureTarget\" -> closureTarget, \n\t  \
\"ClosureAmplitudeRule\" -> closureAmplitudeRule, \n\t  \
\"SampleStableFrequencies\" -> stableFrequencies[12, 1, 1], \n\t  \
\"Pipeline\" -> fullPipeline, \n\t  \"Tests\" -> smallestScaleTestSuite[]\n\t\
|>;\n```\n\nRun the framework summary:\n\n```wl\nsmallestScaleFramework[]\n\
```\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended next refinement steps\n\n### Section A: \
Improve the action\n\nThe current Layer 1 action has no derivative terms, so \
the Euler equations are algebraic rather than PDEs. A more structurally rich \
next step is: $$\\mathcal{L} = \\rho \\log(\\chi) + \\chi R^2 + a_\\rho \
|\\nabla \\rho|^2 + a_\\chi |\\nabla \\chi|^2 + a_R |\\nabla R|^2$$This would \
produce true field equations and enable a meaningful stability spectrum.\n\n\
### Section B: Make toroidal closure geometric\n\nReplace the closure \
functional with an explicit toroidal embedding:\n\n* parameterize a torus in \
$$\\mathbb{R}^3$$,\n\n* define a field on the torus,\n\n* impose periodicity \
and closure residuals,\n\n* compare spherical and toroidal formulations.\n\n\
### Section C: Define \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]smallest stable scale\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] operationally\n\nAt present, stability is only a \
residual notion. We should formalize it by requiring:\n\n1. bounded closure \
residual,\n\n2. positive-definite or semibounded energy,\n\n3. nondivergent \
measurement functional,\n\n4. linearized spectrum without exponentially \
growing modes,\n\n5. reproducibility under precision refinement.\n\n### \
Section D: Only then test particle-like claims\n\nIf you want to investigate \
whether a smallest toroidally closed stable mode resembles a quark-like \
classification, the next stage should compare derived invariants against \
observed quantum numbers rather than assume the identification.\n\n---\n\n## \
14. Suggested execution order\n\nFor the next notebook phase, the best order \
is:\n\n\|01f4ac **Refine Layer 1 into a true PDE action **"}|>, <|
