          "This is a strong Phase 1 scaffold.\n\nIt is careful in the right \
ways: \n* symbolic where possible,\n* explicit about assumptions,\n* modular,\
\n* test-oriented,\n* and importantly, it avoids overclaiming physical \
interpretation.\nA few points stand out as especially good:\n\n1. Your note \
that ``WorkingPrecision -> Infinity`` is not an executable general numeric \
setting in Wolfram Language is correct.\n2. The separation of: \n   * Layer 0 \
seed,\n   * Layer 1 action/stationarity,\n   * Layer 2 measurement,\n   * \
closure/stability tests,\n   is well structured.\n3. The use of \
[SphericalHarmonicY](paclet:ref/SphericalHarmonicY) for the first angular \
basis is mathematically natural, especially given the sphere-based Layer 0 \
seed and the standard spherical Laplacian structure via \
[Laplacian](paclet:ref/Laplacian).\n4. Your warning that the current action \
produces only algebraic stationarity equations is exactly the right \
diagnosis. \n\n## Main correction before Phase 2\n\nYour proposed next step \
is the right one: \n$$\\mathcal{L}\n=\n\\rho \\log(\\chi)\n+\n\\chi R^2\n+\n\
a_\\rho |\\nabla \\rho|^2\n+\na_\\chi |\\nabla \\chi|^2\n+\na_R |\\nabla \
R|^2$$\nThat is the minimum structural change needed to make Step 1 of your \
OPF pipeline actually produce field equations rather than pointwise algebraic \
constraints. \n\n## Recommended Phase 2 scope\n\nI would suggest the next \
notebook should do exactly these four things and no more: \n\n### 1. Replace \
the algebraic action by a derivative action\n\nUse spherical coordinates and \
define the gradient terms explicitly. Since you are already working on a \
sphere/ball, you can use [Laplacian](paclet:ref/Laplacian) in spherical \
coordinates or construct the variational equations directly.\n\nA clean \
symbolic density is: \n\n```wl\nlagrangianDensityPDE[\n\t  rho_, chi_, res_, \
\n\t  gradRhoSq_, gradChiSq_, gradResSq_, \n\t  aRho_, aChi_, aRes_\n\t] := \
rho Log[chi] + chi res ^ 2 + aRho gradRhoSq + aChi gradChiSq + aRes \
gradResSq;\n```\n\nThen derive Euler\[Dash]Lagrange equations for the fields. \
\n\n### 2. Keep the angular basis spectral\n\nExpand fields in spherical \
modes using [SphericalHarmonicY](paclet:ref/SphericalHarmonicY), because on \
the sphere they diagonalize the angular part of the Laplacian: \n\
$$\\Delta_{S^2} Y_{\\ell m} = -\\ell(\\ell+1) Y_{\\ell m}$$\nThis is exactly \
the right basis for a smallest-scale symbolic stability analysis. \n\n### 3. \
Define stability operationally\n\nYour Section C is excellent. I would \
formalize it into predicates such as: \n* ``closureResidualBoundedQ``\n* \
``energySemiboundedQ``\n* ``measurementConvergentQ``\n* \
``noGrowingLinearModesQ``\n* ``precisionStableQ``\nThen the phrase \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]smallest stable scale\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] becomes \
a computable property. \n\n### 4. Delay torus embedding until after PDE \
validation\n\nThis is important. A torus parameterization is useful, but \
introducing it too early can mix: \n* topology,\n* embedding geometry,\n* and \
field dynamics.\nYour current spherical seed plus closure residual is a good \
first symbolic frame. The toroidal embedding should come after the PDE layer \
is validated. \n\n## Best immediate refinement\n\nIf your next notebook is \
